you've made some great videos to help  people learn what do you do with them  I'm Eddie Wu and I teach high school  mathematics in Sydney Australia in 2012  I started putting videos of my everyday  classroom teaching online and in this  video I'm gonna show you how to do the  same  we'll cover making a channel uploading  content and organizing it so it's easier  for people to use and to make a youtube  channel you'll need a Google account if  you already have a personal one I'd  encourage you to make a separate one for  your educational content some schools  and other education organizations like  mine use Google services and so you  might already have a school-based Google  account ready to go click on your  profile up in the top right then go  ahead and create a channel follow the  prompts and before long you'll have your  very own home on YouTube speaking of  which one of the first things you can do  is make it feel a bit more like home  upload a profile picture you like and  find a large wide image to put in place  as your channels header we're about to  upload your first video but before we do  that it's a good idea to go and make  some small changes to your upload  defaults every video on YouTube has  certain information like the title which  helps people find content when they go  searching well when they're watching  similar videos and two of the most  important pieces of information are the  video description and the videos tags  which tell YouTube what topics your  video is connected to back when I  started on YouTube I uploaded dozens of  videos onto my channel before I really  paid attention to these and when I did I  found it kind of cumbersome and  time-consuming to put in similar  information for every single video I put  up especially because I posted so many  videos upload defaults are exactly what  they sound like  we can put some generally relevant  information in here and they'll appear  by default on every video you put up to  edit these upload defaults go from your  channel into YouTube studio find the  settings option and then upload defaults  will be one of the subsections  and some useful information in here and  it'll save you having to put it in over  and over again  each time you upload now it's time to  put up your first video the upload  button can be found near the top right  of the page find the video file in your  computer and is selected to begin the  upload process and give your video a  title that's as succinct as possible but  still gives a helpful description  about the videos content you'll also  notice that the description and tags  have already been filled in with what we  put into the upload defaults but you can  add or change any details that seem  relevant to this video upload speeds  obviously vary widely depending on what  your internet connection is like and how  large your video file is I tend to let  my videos upload in the background while  I get other work done just remember you  have enough power and stay within range  of internet access once your videos  uploaded to YouTube their servers take  over and begin processing your file  again the length of this process varies  depending on how long your video is what  quality is how busy YouTube is with the  hundreds of hours of other content  that's being uploaded every minute of  the day the upload page will let you  know what's processing is finished at  this point your video is ready to go  live but before you do there are a  couple of final things to look out for  first now the video is processed you can  see the thumbnail has been automatically  created from one of the frames in the  video YouTube always gives you three  options to choose from if you don't like  that first one alternatively you can  make your own thumbnail image to upload  and this is often the primary thing that  grabs people's attention when they're  choosing something to watch  personally I don't do this because I  upload too often and if other priorities  for my time but pretty much every  serious YouTube out there puts a lot of  effort into this so I want you to be  aware of how it's done you can always  change this image later if you like the  other thing to keep in mind is  scheduling you can publish a video  immediately or set it to become visible  at a certain time I do this so that my  videos come out at regular intervals  rather than all at once when I squeeze  some time in for editing you may also  want to do this so that students can log  in and watch at a given time for a task  you want them to work on let's make this  one live right now if you head back to  your channels homepage you should see  your brand-new shiny video ready to go  I'd only suggest one more thing for you  at this point which is to consider  adding your video to a playlist this way  you can organize all the videos that  relate to a particular teaching topic  into one place I know this won't seem  all that relevant when you have just a  handful of videos on your channel but  one day you might have a few thousand  and you don't really want to make people  dig through those videos in reverse  chronological order to find the one that  they need  that's it we've covered recording  equipment basic shooting and editing  principles and now you know how to get  onto YouTube and share your videos with  the world now remember watching videos  alone is no substitute for the full  experience of learning with others in an  interactive way from a trained teacher  but if there's one thing I've learned  over the last few years of having my  channel videos can be an enormous leap  our fillet to support student learning  by leveraging all the knowledge and  experience of a skilled educator and  putting that in the hands of students I  hope you found these videos helpful and  they encourage you to kickstart your own  journey in creating a truly global  classroom
 

